{%- comment -%}
Machine-readable author for microformats2 consumers.

The port source has no p-author anywhere, and for a single-author personal
blog that is a defensible omission: its readers already know who wrote it.
This theme is not that. It is a reference theme for a Micropub server, whose
whole purpose is publishing posts that other people's software consumes —
and mf2's author-discovery algorithm has exactly three inputs: p-author on
the h-entry, p-author on the parent h-feed, or rel=author pointing at an
h-card. With none of them, every post this theme publishes is authorless to
Aperture, Monocle, Bridgy and any webmention receiver: no name, no avatar,
no profile link. So this is a deliberate divergence from the source, not a
parity gap in either direction.

Rendered .sr-only rather than as a visible byline: mf2 parsers read the DOM,
not the paint, so the data is fully available to them while the source's
visual design (which has no byline) is preserved exactly.

Emits nothing at all when site.json's author.name is unset, so the neutral
default ships no empty h-card — same conditional discipline as the Atom
feed's author block (eleventy.config.js) and base.liquid's meta author.

eleventy:ignore on the avatar: the eleventy-img transform processes literal
<img> elements, and an author avatar is typically a remote URL (a Gravatar,
an IndieAuth profile photo) that the transform would try to fetch at build
time and fail on. The theme's own content images are unaffected.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if site.author.name -%}<span class="p-author h-card sr-only">{% if site.author.avatar %}<img class="u-photo" src="{{ site.author.avatar }}" alt="" width="48" height="48" eleventy:ignore>{% endif %}{% if site.author.url %}<a class="p-name u-url" href="{{ site.author.url }}">{{ site.author.name }}</a>{% else %}<span class="p-name">{{ site.author.name }}</span>{% endif %}</span>{%- endif -%}
